WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:03.700 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  my sister Fernand and I grew up loving  animals our best family vacation was a  trip to Disney's Animal Kingdom I was  five years old and I went on the safari  right when I saw their reenactment of  poachers trying to steal a baby elephant  my heart beat so hard I still remember  how it found it when the elephant was  rescued this was Disney after all I  cried with relief lil did I know that  one day Fernand and I would have our own  rescue mission six years ago we made a  tiger he was living in a small town in  Mexico in a public park where the  government couldn't afford to pay for  his medication his home was a windowless  concrete heart full of all filthy Hey  the Tigers name was Raja which means  Indian King but Raja was in the sort of  majestic creature you'd expect to see in  a Disney movie he was so weak he  couldn't even stand up he had red bra  open sores on his legs when I looked at  Raja and I really looked into his deep  dark watery eyes I could feel his misery  and all I wanted to do was to take him  out of that concrete prison and find a  place where he could go and be healthy  and safe again trying to find such a  place  Fernanda and I contacted a number of  sanctuaries finally one in color I  agreed to take Raja after countless  meetings and phone calls and paperwork  over an eight week period we finally  convinced the Mexican government to let  go of fragile but before we could do  that transport Raja died but Fernanda  heard the news we held each other and we  decided that we would do everything we  could to prevent other animals from  sharing Rajas unhappy ending in 2015 we  had our first test mexico's Congress  passed a well intentioned law that  banned animal performances from circuses  from one day to another 1,200 animals  needed a new place to live the lack of  suitable places to relocate these  animals resulted in the death of a  thousand of them Fran Anthony started a  social media campaign  we contacted International centuries and  we talked to the Mexican authorities and  then we learned that this was just the  tip of the iceberg in the past 15 years  in Mexico alone 45,000 wild animals have  been victims of abuse exploitation and  wildlife trade and the wounds that were  lucky enough to be rescued didn't have a  safe place to go sadly this is a problem  that goes far beyond Mexico according to  the world's wildlife organization  environmental degradation together with  illegal wildlife trade has led to an  extinction rate between a thousand and  ten thousand times higher than the  natural extinction rate since 1976 we've  wiped out 66  zero percent of the world's wildlife  recently the last male white rhino died  there are still two female white rhinos  in the planet but when they die the  whole species will be gone forever  the heart of the problem lies in the  lack of awareness about wildlife  conservation in some cultures rhino horn  is used as if it was a miracle cure in  others ivory is a symbol of status but  it's not just exotic products that harm  wildlife if you use shampoo or eat ice  cream these everyday products typically  contain palm oil every hour rainforest  area equivalent to 300 soccer fields are  being lost to pump all firming leaving  hundreds of wild animals and endangered  species homeless thankfully most of the  time when people learn about the  negative consequences their behaviors  have they start caring each one of us in  this room can make a difference by  changing some of our behaviors in 2017  Fernand and I decided to build Latin  America's first wildlife sanctuary to  house and rehabilitate abused wild  animals located in 50 acres in the  center of Mexico sand why is being  designed to accommodate 200 wild animals  including bears elephants and big cats  through the best veterinary practices  and top-notch facilities our sanctuary  will provide these animals with a  healthy and safe place to live in  Central visitors will be able to witness  the consequences of illegal wildlife  trade and environmental degradation but  they will also learn ways in which they  can contribute to the solution our end  goal is to replicate this kind of  sanctuaries across the world currently  we're in the process of raising funds  and hopefully in  few months we will be able to start  building the place that will change the  lives of hundreds of wild animals but  most importantly their behaviors and  minds of thousands of visitors however  you don't have to build a wildlife  sanctuary to make a difference minor  changes in your everyday habit could  save the lives of hundreds of wild  animals a few people have asked me why  should I care about saving wild animals  well let me tell you biodiversity is  fundamental for a healthy ecosystem a  healthy ecosystem is fundamental for a  healthy and sustainable economy  these animals are fundamental part of  that biodiversity but mostly I just want  to show them a picture of Raja to me the  reason why we need to save these animals  is self-evident there are many things  you can do to help protect well animals  but today I want you to live with three  in mind first reduce food waste 40% of  the food grown in the planet is wasted  growing that food destroys habitats  so let's not grow so much more than we  need  second by environmentally responsible  products look at the ingredients make  sure they are responsibly sourced  especially if it contains palm oil third  reduce meat consumption try to shift  from red to white meat the livestock  industry is responsible for a  disproportionate amount of annual  habitat destruction  then you can always volunteer or donate  to sanctuaries like mine today I feel  very lucky to be part of the GSB  community people like you have the power  to inspire thousands of others through  the companies you will create through  the policies you will influence and  through the children you will raise  let's all work together in creating  real-life happy endings for animals like  preju thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

